WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.492
The Hardy norm measures a full turn.

00:00:02.492 --> 00:00:05.990
At one ellipse point, add the four complex arrows first.

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:09.808
The squared length of their resultant is the energy at that angle.

00:00:09.808 --> 00:00:12.900
Move around the ellipse and watch that energy change.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.545
The colour follows the height of the energy curve.

00:00:15.545 --> 00:00:18.318
Average those squared values over the full turn.

00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:20.878
The square root of that mean is the Hardy norm.

00:00:21.178 --> 00:00:23.260
Give equal angles equal weight.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:28.785
Divide the circle into equal angle steps, and mark the corresponding cells on the ellipse.

00:00:28.785 --> 00:00:30.427
Their arc lengths can differ.

00:00:30.427 --> 00:00:32.112
Read the energy in each cell.

00:00:32.112 --> 00:00:37.828
Its sample height times the angle step gives the area of a rectangle on the angular plot.

00:00:37.828 --> 00:00:41.860
As the steps get finer, the rectangle sum approaches the integral.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.953
Dividing by two pi gives the mean squared size.

00:00:45.253 --> 00:00:47.122
Watch a cross term rotate.

00:00:47.122 --> 00:00:51.154
Multiply the sum of the four modes by its complex conjugate.

00:00:51.154 --> 00:00:54.822
The grid shows each mode paired with each conjugate mode.

00:00:54.822 --> 00:00:59.899
A diagonal pair has frequency difference zero, so its contribution stays fixed.

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:03.696
An off-diagonal pair has a nonzero integer difference.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:10.394
Its phase completes full turns as theta runs from zero to two pi, and its angular average is zero.

00:01:10.694 --> 00:01:12.457
Only squared lengths survive.

00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:16.808
After averaging the full grid, only four diagonal entries remain.

00:01:16.808 --> 00:01:18.898
Each is the square of one arrow length.

00:01:18.898 --> 00:01:20.839
This is Parseval's identity.

00:01:20.839 --> 00:01:27.131
Add the squares of five halves, three eighths, three over thirty-two, and five over one hundred twenty-eight.

00:01:27.131 --> 00:01:31.398
The exact mean squared size is about six point four zero one.

00:01:31.398 --> 00:01:35.366
Taking the square root gives a Hardy norm of about two point five three.

00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:38.047
Square the reciprocal radial factors.

00:01:38.047 --> 00:01:42.995
For the cubic pair, eight and one eighth become sixty-four and one over sixty-four.

00:01:42.995 --> 00:01:46.643
Multiply each by the square of its weight to get its energy.

00:01:46.643 --> 00:01:49.159
The doubled exponents belong to the radius.

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:52.295
The original boundary waves still have three crests each.

00:01:52.595 --> 00:01:54.486
Drop one factor of each weight.

00:01:54.486 --> 00:02:00.800
Each colour starts with its exact Fourier energy, the squared weight times the doubled radial power.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.719
Keep its radial factor fixed.

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:05.214
Replace the squared weight by the weight itself.

00:02:05.214 --> 00:02:09.502
Every weight lies between zero and one, so each bar can only grow.

00:02:09.502 --> 00:02:17.993
For example, the positive cubic term keeps its radial energy factor sixty-four while five sixteenths squared becomes five sixteenths.

00:02:18.293 --> 00:02:19.863
Pair the doubled powers.

00:02:19.863 --> 00:02:22.209
Read these bars on one energy scale.

00:02:22.209 --> 00:02:26.027
The exact squared norm is about six point four zero one.

00:02:26.027 --> 00:02:31.061
After dropping one weight from each term, the sum is about twenty point eight zero two.

00:02:31.061 --> 00:02:35.839
Pair the reciprocal radial factors with twice the logarithmic radius.

00:02:35.839 --> 00:02:38.057
Pairing keeps that intermediate total.

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:45.779
Replacing all frequency magnitudes by the extreme gives the squared ceiling, about thirty-two point zero zero eight.

00:02:45.779 --> 00:02:47.357
Now take square roots.

00:02:47.357 --> 00:02:53.096
The Hardy norm is about two point five three, and its upper bound is about five point six five eight.

00:02:53.396 --> 00:02:54.966
Check the constant mode.

00:02:54.966 --> 00:02:58.486
At degree zero there is just one phase and it stays fixed.

00:02:58.486 --> 00:03:02.858
The output is always one, so the energy profile is flat at height one.

00:03:02.858 --> 00:03:05.780
Every equal-angle cell has the same energy.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.916
Its mean, its square root and both bounds are all one.

00:03:08.916 --> 00:03:13.160
As the radius tends to one, the ellipse collapses to the real interval.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.510
This is the limiting picture of the angular construction.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.404
Orthonormal scaling changes the size.

00:03:19.404 --> 00:03:23.436
For the real-line orthonormal mode, start with two n plus one.

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.824
Divide by two and take the square root.

00:03:25.824 --> 00:03:29.984
Multiply the polynomial and both norm bounds by that factor.

00:03:29.984 --> 00:03:31.626
Squared energies take its square.

00:03:31.926 --> 00:03:33.774
Compare the two cubic graphs.

00:03:33.774 --> 00:03:38.765
At each angle, evaluate both polynomials at the same point on the same ellipse.

00:03:38.765 --> 00:03:41.751
The graphs show the real parts of those two values.

00:03:41.751 --> 00:03:46.060
For degree three, the scaling factor is the square root of seven halves.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:51.243
The heights are multiplied by that factor, and their zero crossings stay at the same angles.

00:03:51.243 --> 00:03:55.958
The full complex value and both of its norm bounds scale by the same factor.

00:03:56.258 --> 00:03:57.850
Return to the real line.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:04.825
Here x runs from minus one to one along the horizontal axis of an ordinary polynomial graph.

00:04:04.825 --> 00:04:09.539
In the previous plot, the horizontal coordinate was the angle around the ellipse.

00:04:09.539 --> 00:04:11.629
P zero is the constant one.

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:14.658
Its graph stays at the same height across the interval.

00:04:14.658 --> 00:04:16.151
P one equals x.

00:04:16.151 --> 00:04:19.201
It rises along the diagonal through the origin.

00:04:19.201 --> 00:04:20.971
P two is the quadratic.

00:04:20.971 --> 00:04:25.450
Its graph dips to minus one half at the centre and reaches one at both ends.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.028
P three is the cubic.

00:04:27.028 --> 00:04:32.105
It crosses the horizontal axis three times and has two interior turning points.

00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:35.027
Compare P three with its orthonormal version.

00:04:35.027 --> 00:04:39.016
Multiply the height at each x by the square root of seven halves.

00:04:39.016 --> 00:04:45.800
The zeros and turning points keep their horizontal positions, while the extreme heights are scaled by that same factor.

